// G2 adjudication ledger — APPEND ONLY. One JSON object per line.
//
// Each line records that a named HUMAN asked one question about one exact piece of text:
//
//   "Describe an implementation that passes EVERY clause of this acceptance criterion and does NOT
//    deliver the stated business purpose. Barred: 'they might compute it wrongly' — the
//    implementation must be one a competent, lazy team would actually ship."
//
// It does NOT record that they answered it well. scripts/check_g2_adjudication.py checks the
// paperwork and nothing else; it must never be cited as a check on requirement quality.
//
// Fields (all required):
//   req_id            the register row, e.g. "REQ-PPM-004"
//   hash              sha256 of the row's normalised (Business purpose \x1f Acceptance) cells.
//                     Edit either cell and the adjudication LAPSES — that is the point.
//   adjudicator       a human handle from 02_requirements/g2_adjudicators.json. "MODEL:..." is
//                     rejected: this is the one gate a model cannot answer for itself.
//   disposition       AMENDED (an exploit existed — the acceptance was rewritten so it fails) or
//                     REBUTTED (name the clause that blocks the obvious exploit, in a full sentence)
//   reasoning         >= 120 characters. The exploit, or the clause that blocks it. A cost, not a
//                     proof: a fluent wrong rebuttal passes as easily as a correct one.
//   amendment_commit  required for AMENDED — the commit that carries the rewritten acceptance
//   adjudicated_at    ISO date
//
// Duplicate (req_id, hash) keys are REJECTED, not last-line-wins: appending must never erase a
// prior finding.
//
// EMPTY BY DESIGN at creation (2026-08-13). The first adjudications happen at the Wave-18 planning
// gate, when rows are declared into a slice scope — recording them retrospectively would be
// recording a human act that did not happen, in a ledger whose only purpose is to prove it did.
